 "literal": "\"Pāri, Pāri,\" saying many times, praising, / one person they praise, the eloquent-tongued poets. / Pāri is not the only one. / Rain also exists here, to protect the world!",
 "translation": "Poets with eloquent tongues praise a single man, crying out \"Pāri! Pāri!\" many times. But Pāri is not the only one who gives; there is also the rain here to protect and sustain the world!",
 "modern": "\"பாரி, பாரி\" என்று பலமுறை துதித்துப் பாடி, செந்நாப் புலவர்கள் ஒருவனையே புகழ்கிறார்கள். பாரி ஒருவன் மட்டும் கொடை அளிப்பவன் அல்லன்; இவ்வுலகைக் காப்பதற்கு மழையும் இங்கே இருக்கிறது அல்லவா!",
 "explanation": "This poem is composed by Kabilar in praise of his patron and close friend, the generous chieftain Vēḷ Pāri. Kabilar uses a beautiful device of mock-criticism to elevate Pāri's fame. He gently chides his fellow poets for praising only Pāri as the sole provider, pointing out that the rain also exists to sustain the world. By comparing Pāri's boundless generosity to the life-giving rain, Kabilar suggests that Pāri's giving is as natural, effortless, and essential to the world as the rain itself. The key words are 'pāri' (the chieftain), 'pulavar' (poets), and 'māri' (rain), establishing a poetic equivalence between the patron and nature's greatest gift.",
